The period from 1606 to 1648 covers the four decades between the arrival of the Baiduist exiles and the outbreak of the [[Sinchew War]]. It is the most violent period in the colony's history and the one in which its modern population was formed.

More than a million people were transported to Sinchew in the years after 1606, arriving without notice in a colony whose population in 1605 was about 84,000. The consequence was famine, dispossession and a society divided on religious lines. In the same decades the colony purchased frontier land from [[Hachinawa]] to settle the arrivals, and joined Hachinawa in the [[Third Mahik War]] of 1614 to 1618, which transferred the [[Timara]] country to the two of them.

== Famine Era (1606–1619) ==

[[Tân Khéng-hui]] (陳慶輝) was governor from 1606 to 1619. He received the exiles and governed through the famines of 1607, 1609 and 1611.

The [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong|expulsion]] was directed at Sinchew rather than at [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]], and the court's reasons were several.

The first was the colony's manner of governing itself. The governorship of Sinchew had come to be filled by nomination of the colony's own assembly, which submitted a name for appointment by the crown. The practice had been tolerated and had become established, but it was not regarded at the court as a proper arrangement: it made the governor the nominee of the governed, and it had come about by the accident of a vacancy during the [[Great Peasant Revolt (Zong)|Great Peasant Revolt]] rather than by grant.

The second was the part the colony was believed to have played in the disorders of the mainland. The growth of the plantation economy in Sinchew and in Louyang had flooded the Zong market with colonial goods, ruined producers in the northern provinces, and contributed to the conditions of the Great Peasant Revolt of 1572 to 1585. The colonies profited from the shortage their own goods had produced, and the court attributed the disorders of the mainland in part to them.

The third was the colony's conduct during the [[Baiduist usurpation]]. When [[Foyuan of Zong|Foyuan]] took the throne, the colonial government at Tamsui did not at once refuse his authority. Other administrations did, and were able to plead their fidelity afterwards; Sinchew had waited, and its delay was remembered.

The fourth was the colony's connection with the rebellion that finally destroyed the Canglan cause. The [[Canglan Rebellion]] of about 1605 had been financed in part by Baiduist merchant houses holding property in Sinchew, and the property of those houses lay within a colony that had been slow to renounce the usurper.

The transportation served two purposes. It punished a colony suspected of independence and of disloyalty, and it removed from the mainland a population whose religion the court had resolved to extirpate. The transportation was ordered as a single measure and the record treats it as one.

The first ships arrived without announcement. The colonial administration at Tamsui had no notice of the numbers to be landed, no provision for their reception, and no authority over the process by which they were carried.

The returns of 1605 record a population of about 84,000. More than a million people were landed over the following decades. The colony's administration had no means of managing an arrival of that scale.

=== Arrival ===

Famine followed in 1607, 1609 and 1611. The colony's agriculture could not feed the population that had been landed on it, the arrivals had no land and no means of obtaining it, and the transport that had carried them continued to be directed from Zong rather than from the colony itself.

The famines of these years were the first of a series. They established a pattern of the period: a population exceeding what the colony could provision, sustained by imports and by the opening of new land, and failing when either stopped.

=== Population and settlement ===

The colony's population was transformed.

The exiles were Baiduist, and the colony's administration was Tianist and remained so, because the crown appointed it and the crown had made Baiduism the object of the expulsion. The result was a two-tier society on religious lines. The Tianist minority held the offices, the courts and the grants of land; the Baiduist majority held neither, and was settled wherever circumstances permitted rather than where the administration would have chosen.

The settlement of the arrivals was concentrated on land the administration did not want, which is to say land unsuitable for the plantation economy. The largest such settlement was along the shores of [[Tn̂g-ô͘]], the great northern lake, whose steep margins were fit for neither cash crops nor paddy.

=== Censuses ===

The administration continued to take a return every five years, and the classes it recorded were altered to reflect the population it now governed. The four classes of the period before 1606 - free Zong, indentured Zong, native slaves and free natives - no longer described a colony in which the principal division was between the Tianist administration and the Baiduist exiles.

The returns from 1610 record four classes: Tianist, Baiduist, enslaved, and free natives.

