The Tianist population grew slowly, from 47,818 to 87,592 over forty years, and remained a small minority throughout. The Baiduist population, which does not appear in the return of 1605, reached 1,306,748 by 1645, and the whole of the colony's growth in the period is attributable to it.

The free native population reversed its long decline. It had fallen in every return between 1540 and 1605, from 4,197 in 1540 to 1,032 in 1605. From 1610 it increased, reaching 48,619 by 1645. The increase has two causes: natural growth, and the movement of Hachina cultivators onto the frontier land their own court had sold to the colony. The land sales transferred people as well as land, and the class that had been shrinking for a century began to grow as a consequence of a transaction the administration had entered into for another purpose.

The enslaved population grew at about one per cent a year throughout, against three and four per cent in the preceding period, and the difference is the end of importation rather than a change in the condition of the people already held. The traffic of the [[Secret Trade]] did not survive the arrival. The colony now contained more than a million labourers who had been landed without purchase, and their competition made the cost of procuring, carrying and maintaining a captive from [[Dagit]] higher than the cost of employing a Baiduist. The trade that had run for seventy years unlawfully was ended by the price of labour rather than by any enforcement of the law against it. Some vessels continued to arrive, and the returns to 1645 imply a small and diminishing traffic.

The famines of 1607, 1609 and 1611 are visible in the returns as a rate rather than as an entry. The return of 1610 records 267,804 Baiduists, against the number the transport would have delivered by that year, and the difference is the mortality of the three famines.

=== Baiduists in Hachinawa ===

Not all of the exiles remained in the colony. Numbers of Baiduists made their way to [[Hachinawa]], where the empire had been buying frontier land from the colony in the same years and where Baiduist communities took up cultivation on their own account.

They do not appear in the colony's returns, and their number is not recorded in the colonial record. The Hachina account treats them as settlers within the empire rather than as refugees, and their presence is one of several ways in which the expulsion altered the country that received the colony's land.

== Land Purchase Era (1619–1635) ==

[[Lîm Bûn-giâm]] (林文嚴) was governor from 1619 to 1635. The purchase of Hachina frontier land and the Third Mahik War fall within his term.

Land was bought from Hachinawa, whose court had been selling frontier parcels since the 1580s.

Hachinawa's gold revenue had been failing since the end of the sixteenth century, and its court had been selling frontier land since the 1580s. The transfer of more than a million people to a colony on its northern frontier gave it a buyer with an urgent need and the means to pay, and the sales of the period were on a larger scale than those of the preceding decades.

Hachinawa gained from the sales in three ways. It sold land at prices set by its own need rather than the buyer's, it acquired grain exports to a market that could not feed itself, and it obtained from the colony in the same years the support of a military campaign.

=== Third Mahik War ===

{{Main|Third Mahik War}}

The area to the south and south-east of Hachinawa was held by [[Timara]] chiefdoms rather than by a single polity, and the colony and the empire agreed in 1614 to divide it between them. The war lasted four years and ended in their favour.

The settlement transferred the Timara country to the two victors and was concluded by the Treaty of Tn̂g-ô͘ of 1618, which fixed the frontier between the colony and the empire. Under the boundary established in 1618, Hachinawa held about 1,581,000 km² and the colony about 600,000 km².

For Hachinawa the conquest was the largest single extension of its territory, and it was obtained without payment by an empire that had been selling land for forty years.

== Wartime Approach (1635–1648) ==

[[Ng Tek-seng]] (黃德生) was governor from 1635 to 1648. He declined the proposal of the [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] administration to invade [[Hachinawa]], and was governor when the [[Sinchew War]] began in 1648.

=== Louyang ===

The colony of [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] had spent the same decades advancing northward along the [[Tsuwasha]], and by the 1610s had reached the river's upper course on Hachinawa's southern frontier.

Louyang petitioned the Sinchew administration in secret to join it in an invasion of Hachinawa. The proposal was to divide the empire between the two colonies, and Louyang's interest in it was that the territory it wanted lay between itself and the sea.

The administration at Tamsui declined. Its judgement was that Hachinawa would not be able to resist the military power of Zong, and that the invasion would therefore succeed — but that the colony's own population, already exceeded by its obligations, was too small to hold what it took. The proposal was not refused on principle; it was deferred.

The rivalry was one between two colonial administrations of the same power. Their interests had diverged since the [[Tamsui Plot]] of 1543, when the territory was divided between them, and the division of Hachinawa would have settled the question of which was the senior colony on the western coast.

=== Hachina's passage to the east ===

The empire had depended since the sixteenth century on the goodwill of the [[Yelani]] chiefdoms of the mountains for any trade with the [[Aethiopean Ocean|Aethiopean]] coast, because no route crossed the mountains that a trading party could take without them.

By 1610 Hachinawa had established a controlled route to the eastern seaboard, and with it direct commerce with the [[Jacano|Jacanese]] and [[Mercians|Mercian]] traders who had ports on that coast. The new harbour, [[Kayahatala]], was the empire's first port on the second ocean, and it ended the dependence on the Yelani that had set the terms of the eastern policy for seventy years.

The effect on the colony was unwelcome. Hachinawa had been the market the colony sold grain to and the source of the land it settled on. An empire with a port of its own, dealing directly with other Illypnian powers, was an empire with alternatives.

=== Baiduist Revolutionary Army ===

The army that began the [[Sinchew War]] in 1648 was not formed for the purpose. It began as a religious and mutual-aid society, and it became an army over the course of two decades.

The Ocean Crossing Society (海渡會, Hái-tō͘-hōe) was founded in the 1620s among the Baiduist settlements of the interior by Chiu Hoat-bêng (周法明), a monk of the exiles. Its stated objects were the preservation of Baiduist practice and the provision of mutual support among the arrivals, and its functions were those of a religious association: it maintained the observance of festivals and the ordination of monks, collected contributions for the relief of the destitute, and provided for burial and for the support of widows and orphans within the community.

Two measures of the colonial administration produced it. The first was taxation. The exiles were assessed for the support of an administration that excluded them from office and from land grants, and the assessment fell on a population that had arrived with nothing. The second was residence. The administration required the Baiduists to live outside [[Tamsui]], and the settlements they established in the interior were outside the reach of the colony's ordinary government — which is to say, in places where a voluntary society could operate with less interference and more effect than the administration's own officers.

The society grew because the need was real. It supplied what the administration did not: relief in the famines, arbitration between households, the education of children and the burial of the dead. By the 1630s it had branches in most of the Baiduist settlements, and its officers were the men to whom the community turned rather than to the officials of the colony.

The transition to an armed body followed the settlement of the lake. The concentration of the exiles along the margins of [[Tn̂g-ô͘]] produced a population that was numerous, poor, excluded and geographically collected — conditions in which a society with branches, officers and a membership already organised for mutual aid required only arms and a decision. The society acquired both in the 1640s.

The name by which it fought was adopted at the outbreak of the war. The Ocean Crossing Society was not dissolved; it was the body from which the revolutionary army was raised, and its officers became its commanders. The administration at Tamsui had tolerated it for twenty years as a charitable association, and had registered its branches, and did not treat it as a political body until it had one.

By the 1640s the colony's difficulties had compounded. The population continued to arrive and continued to exceed what the colony could feed; the Baiduist majority remained excluded from land and office; and the settlement of the exiles along the margins of [[Tn̂g-ô͘]] had produced a concentration of population in one region, on land nobody else wanted, and among people who had no reason to be loyal to the administration that had put them there.

The [[Sinchew War]] began in 1648. The Baiduist revolutionary army was raised along the lake, and the colony's administration found itself fighting a population it had been given, on land it had bought, with a treasury it had spent on grain.

It was in the opening years of that war that the administration at Tamsui first asked Hachinawa for assistance in a domestic matter, and the request was made of a power the colony had been selling to for forty years and planning to invade for thirty.

